{"parentUuid":"9ced4c44-f91b-4632-ab12-d8c272a62f13","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgt9eWzJEzY4qxH9LmvYV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Here is what I'd borrow from the well-known leaders. It's from memory of established practice, not fetched pages, so I can pull current sources to confirm any detail you want to rely on.\n\n**What the best do**\n- **Keep a Changelog:** write for humans, not machines, and never dump the git log. Group by what it means to the reader (Added, Changed, Deprecated, Removed, Fixed, Security), newest first, with the date.\n- **Kubernetes:** the author writes the release note in the PR, in a required block, and reviewers review it with the code. A change that needs no note says so explicitly (\"NONE\"). Entries that need user action are called out as \"action required\" and surfaced at the top.\n- **Stripe's API changelog:** each entry says what changed, who is affected and what to do. Breaking and non-breaking changes are marked, and migration guidance sits next to the change.\n- **Rust and Go release notes:** the headline features get a few plain sentences, and often a short before and after example, grouped by area. Compatibility notes live in their own section.\n- **Linear and Vercel:** lead with the benefit, keep it short, and show the thing.\n- **Changesets-style tools:** the summary is addressed to consumers, and a major bump must carry migration guidance.\n- **Cautionary tale:** changelogs generated from commit messages read like developer logs. That's what ours risked becoming.\n\n**What that means for Callisto**\n1. **Write the changeset in the PR and review it there.** Kubernetes treats the note as part of the change. We could add a \"Changelog\" line to the PR template, with \"no changeset needed\" as an explicit choice.\n2. **Lead with the benefit, then the detail.** The first line is what a user would say to a colleague, and bullets hold the specifics.\n3. **Mark who must act.** `Breaking:` and `Action:` already exist. The lesson is to make them the norm for anything that changes behavior, not an afterthought.\n4. **Show, don't just tell, for headline features.** One before and after snippet beats a paragraph.\n5. **Tier the release.** Highlights for the one or two things people should know, details below. 0.19.0 would have a single highlight: a much better release PR.\n6. **Keep a single voice.** Pick one tense and mood, for example \"Adds…\", \"Fixes…\", \"Now…\", so the changelog reads as one document.\n7. **Don't list fix-ups to unreleased work.** The cleanest changelogs only describe what a user can see.\n\nI'd fold 2, 3, 6 and 7 into the changeset shape in AGENTS.md, with real before and after examples. I'd treat 1 and 5 as separate, bigger decisions for you.\n\nneeds input: which of these do you want in the shape we write down, and the three answers from before: audience, length target, and whether you want a soft lint in Callisto."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68,"cache_read_input_tokens":322816,"output_tokens":2092,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1164},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2092,"cache_read_input_tokens":322816,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":68},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[{"type":"thinking_dropped","path":"messages.2.content.0","reason":"prefix_binding_mismatch"}],"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"previous_message_not_found"}},"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"22cbf274-70a4-4075-8497-72aae335a9