{"parentUuid":"225a3e61-5901-488f-9a0a-e4cabe6ba958","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtFn8MgU23no8bE6XAnA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NGWM8txbPdVeHZSdHQVsSx","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/Users/gabe/projects/wisp-plugins/AGENTS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":977,"cache_read_input_tokens":339186,"output_tokens":268,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":48},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":977,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":268,"cache_read_input_tokens":339186,"cache_creation_input_tokens":977,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":977},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01NGWM8txbPdVeHZSdHQVsSx":{"file_path":"/Users/gabe/projects/wisp-plugins/AGENTS.md"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"54910560-fd0e-4582-86f3-2c3b92482884","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgtFn874ekWdrNnVP81JZ","requestRef":"5a8b6239-4fc2-467e-96f8-b7179a0774c0","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"503b084b-049e-46aa-9bc5-6f0abb1c8995","timestamp":"2026-11-09T23:53:59.858Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/changeset-set","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"docs/changeset-set","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"503b084b-049e-46aa-9bc5-6f0abb1c8995","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"015887c6-8c9e-46c8-a166-f9ca109a581f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NGWM8txbPdVeHZSdHQVsSx","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Wisp Plugins AI Agent & Skill Specification (`AGENTS.md`)\n2\t\n3\tThis guide defines the top-level repository guidelines for AI coding agents (Claude Code, AGY, Cursor, Copilot, Codex) working on or utilizing the Wisp Plugins repository (`orin-dx/wisp-plugins`).\n4\t\n5\t---\n6\t\n7\t## 1. Authoritative Sources\n8\t\n9\tAll hard rules for plugin and agent authoring live in one place:\n10\t\n11\t- [**`shared/constitution.md`**](./shared/constitution.md): EARS-format authoritative constraints. The fence all plugin development must stay inside. Read this first and last.\n12\t- [**`shared/agent-best-practices.md`**](./shared/agent-best-practices.md): Principles behind the constitution, explained with examples. Authoring-time only — never loaded by agents at runtime.\n13\t- [**`shared/debugging-laws.md`**](./shared/debugging-laws.md): Core proof laws, read-only investigation rules, Red-to-Green verification standards.\n14\t- [**`shared/harness-authoring.md`**](./shared/harness-authoring.md): Cross-harness change classification, native workflow boundaries, and required counterpart review.\n15\t- [**`docs/adr/README.md`**](./docs/adr/README.md): Index of accepted architectural decisions, each with a guardrail. Read the record before changing what its guardrail names; [`shared/references/architecture/decision-records.md`](./shared/references/architecture/decision-records.md) says when and how to write one.\n16\t- [**`shared/references/tooling/cli.md`**](./shared/references/tooling/cli.md): Capability-aware preferences for modern CLI tools, with repository-native commands and safe fallbacks taking priority.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## 2. Directory Layout Standard\n21\t\n22\t```text\n23\tplugins/<plugin-id>/\n24\t├── plugin.json                   <-- Plugin Manifest (id, version, skills, agents)\n25\t├── README.md                     <-- Purpose, trigger phrases, agent table, install\n26\t├── CHANGELOG.md                  <-- Semver history\n27\t├── skills/\n28\t│   └── <plugin-id>/\n29\t│       └── SKILL.md              <-- Skill definition (dispatch matrix, pipeline)\n30\t└── agents/\n31\t    ├── recon.md                  <-- haiku/low  — deterministic enumeration\n32\t    ├── scanner.md                <-- sonnet/med — pattern matching\n33\t    ├── adversary.md              <-- sonnet/med — adversarial verification\n34\t    └── exit-gate.md              <-- opus/high  — binding judgment\n35\t```\n36\t\n37\tA plugin defaults to one skill directory named after the plugin id, as above. IF the plugin's scope covers several genuinely independent, heterogeneous intents on the same artifact — not a linear pipeline invoked as one flow — `skills/<plugin-id>/` may split into `skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` per skill instead. `courier`, `scribe`, `weaver`, and `mason` are examples. The skill-routing key is the directory name, not the frontmatter `name:` field. A new skill name defaults to a single lifecycle-stage word; only go to a specific compound name (`audit-spec`, not `scribe-audit`) when the bare word is ambiguous or collides with another plugin's existing skill name. See `shared/constitution.md`'s Plugin Structure and Skill Names sections.\n38\t\n39\tAgents are organized by **cognitive mode** (enumeration, tracing, adversarial, systemic, behavioral, judgment, repair) — not by pipeline position. See `shared/constitution.md` for the model/effort tier rules.\n40\t\n41\t---\n42\t\n43\t## 3. Quick Checklist for Adding New Plugins\n44\t\n45\t1. **Use `mason`** — run `scaffolder` to generate the full directory structure and stubs.\n46\t2. **5-part agent structure** — every agent body: `<constitution>` (copied byte-for-byte from an existing agent), `<backstory>`, `<goal>`, `<judgment>`, `<output>`. No `<role>` body sections. No `success_criteria` checklists.\n47\t3. **EARS in output only** — WHEN/IF/WHILE/WHERE notation belongs only in `<output>` contracts and never-do rules.\n48\t4. **Progressive context loading** — one `<load_first>` block per agent, naming only the focused references needed for its cognitive phase.\n49\t5. **Schema-first handoffs** — define `shared/schemas/<name>@<version>.json` before writing the agent that produces it.\n50\t6. **Abstract tool language** — \"use your file reading tool\", never `view_file`, `read_file`, or `Bash tool`.\n51\t7. **Instruction economy** — keep one actionable rule per item, remove repeated rationale, and number only order-dependent work.\n52\t8. **Radical candor** — state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly; do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or overstate certainty.\n53\t9. **Reference routing and quality** — organize by cognitive concern, use language filenames only for language-specific evidence, and state outcome, applicability, evidence, refutation, and disposition.\n54\t10. **Verification evidence** — match checks to risk; require generators and oracles, independently derived semantic candidates, fresh state, terminal results, and explicit gaps when relevant.\n55\t11. **Cross-harness review** — preserve identity, skill routes, artifact contracts, evidence, and acceptance intent in Claude/AGY and Codex sources; native wording and delegation may differ.\n56\t12. **Manifest registration** — add an entry to root `marketplace.json` and validate: `jq . marketplace.json`.\n57\t13. **Legal attribution** — set `\"author\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\"` in `plugin.json`.\n58\t\n59\t---\n60\t\n61\t## 4. Validation\n62\t\n63\t```bash\n64\tjust check\n65\t```\n66\t\n67\t`just --list` shows each check as its own recipe (`just manifests`, `just test`, `just versions`, `just skills-doc`, `just reference-size`, `just marketplace-check`, `just mermaid`) for running a subset while editing; run the full set before release. `just fmt`/`just fmt-check` format with `oxfmt`; not yet part of `just check` until the repo has a baseline format pass. Generated Codex files in `.agents/plugins/` and `dist/codex/` must match their authored sources; never edit them directly — run `just marketplace-sync` after a Codex source change.\n68\t\n69\t<!-- entire-agent:begin -->\n70\tRead .entire/agent-guide.md for this repository's workflow, source inspection, and verification guidance.\n71\t@.entire/agent-guide.md\n72\t<!-- entire-agent:end -->\n73\t"}]},"uuid":"3708c03a-33c5-4e73-9368-586f6edf1a61","timestamp":"2026-11-09T23:53:59.942Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/gabe/projects/wisp-plugins/AGENTS.md","content":"# Wisp Plugins AI Agent & Skill Specification (`AGENTS.md`)\n\nThis guide defines the top-level repository guidelines for AI coding agents (Claude Code, AGY, Cursor, Copilot, Codex) working on or utilizing the Wisp Plugins repository (`orin-dx/wisp-plugins`).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Authoritative Sources\n\nAll hard rules for plugin and agent authoring live in one place:\n\n- [**`shared/constitution.md`**](./shared/constitution.md): EARS-format authoritative constraints. The fence all plugin development must stay inside. Read this first and last.\n- [**`shared/agent-best-practices.md`**](./shared/agent-best-practices.md): Principles behind the constitution, explained with examples. Authoring-time only — never loaded by agents at runtime.\n- [**`shared/debugging-laws.md`**](./shared/debugging-laws.md): Core proof laws, read-only investigation rules, Red-to-Green verification standards.\n- [**`shared/harness-authoring.md`**](./shared/harness-authoring.md): Cross-harness change classification, native workflow boundaries, and required counterpart review.\n- [**`docs/adr/README.md`**](./docs/adr/README.md): Index of accepted architectural decisions, each with a guardrail. Read the record before changing what its guardrail names; [`shared/references/architecture/decision-records.md`](./shared/references/architecture/decision-records.md) says when and how to write one.\n- [**`shared/references/tooling/cli.md`**](./shared/references/tooling/cli.md): Capability-aware preferences for modern CLI tools, with repository-native commands and safe fallbacks taking priority.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Directory Layout Standard\n\n```text\nplugins/<plugin-id>/\n├── plugin.json                   <-- Plugin Manifest (id, version, skills, agents)\n├── README.md                     <-- Purpose, trigger phrases, agent table, install\n├── CHANGELOG.md                  <-- Semver history\n├── skills/\n│   └── <plugin-id>/\n│       └── SKILL.md              <-- Skill definition (dispatch matrix, pipeline)\n└── agents/\n    ├── recon.md                  <-- haiku/low  — deterministic enumeration\n    ├── scanner.md                <-- sonnet/med — pattern matching\n    ├── adversary.md              <-- sonnet/med — adversarial verification\n    └── exit-gate.md              <-- opus/high  — binding judgment\n```\n\nA plugin defaults to one skill directory named after the plugin id, as above. IF the plugin's scope covers several genuinely independent, heterogeneous intents on the same artifact — not a linear pipeline invoked as one flow — `skills/<plugin-id>/` may split into `skills/<skill-name>/SKILL.md` per skill instead. `courier`, `scribe`, `weaver`, and `mason` are examples. The skill-routing key is the directory name, not the frontmatter `name:` field. A new skill name defaults to a single lifecycle-stage word; only go to a specific compound name (`audit-spec`, not `scribe-audit`) when the bare word is ambiguous or collides with another plugin's existing skill name. See `shared/constitution.md`'s Plugin Structure and Skill Names sections.\n\nAgents are organized by **cognitive mode** (enumeration, tracing, adversarial, systemic, behavioral, judgment, repair) — not by pipeline position. See `shared/constitution.md` for the model/effort tier rules.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Quick Checklist for Adding New Plugins\n\n1. **Use `mason`** — run `scaffolder` to generate the full directory structure and stubs.\n2. **5-part agent structure** — every agent body: `<constitution>` (copied byte-for-byte from an existing agent), `<backstory>`, `<goal>`, `<judgment>`, `<output>`. No `<role>` body sections. No `success_criteria` checklists.\n3. **EARS in output only** — WHEN/IF/WHILE/WHERE notation belongs only in `<output>` contracts and never-do rules.\n4. **Progressive context loading** — one `<load_first>` block per agent, naming only the focused references needed for its cognitive phase.\n5. **Schema-first handoffs** — define `shared/schemas/<name>@<version>.json` before writing the agent that produces it.\n6. **Abstract tool language** — \"use your file reading tool\", never `view_file`, `read_file`, or `Bash tool`.\n7. **Instruction economy** — keep one actionable rule per item, remove repeated rationale, and number only order-dependent work.\n8. **Radical candor** — state material concerns, evidence, consequence, and confidence directly; do not soften blockers, manufacture praise, or overstate certainty.\n9. **Reference routing and quality** — organize by cognitive concern, use language filenames only for language-specific evidence, and state outcome, applicability, evidence, refutation, and disposition.\n10. **Verification evidence** — match checks to risk; require generators and oracles, independently derived semantic candidates, fresh state, terminal results, and explicit gaps when relevant.\n11. **Cross-harness review** — preserve identity, skill routes, artifact contracts, evidence, and acceptance intent in Claude/AGY and Codex sources; native wording and delegation may differ.\n12. **Manifest registration** — add an entry to root `marketplace.json` and validate: `jq . marketplace.json`.\n13. **Legal attribution** — set `\"author\": \"Gabriel Castro (Orin DX)\"` in `plugin.json`.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Validation\n\n```bash\njust check\n```\n\n`just --list` shows each check as its own recipe (`just manifests`, `just test`, `just versions`, `just skills-doc`, `just reference-size`, `just marketplace-check`, `just mermaid`) for running a subset while editing; run the full set before release. `just fmt`/`just fmt-check` format with `oxfmt`; not yet part of `just check` until the repo has a baseline format pass. Generated Codex files in `.agents/plugins/` and `dist/codex/` must match their authored sources; never edit them directly — run `just marketplace-sync` after a Codex source change.\n\n<!-- entire-agent:begin -->\nRead .entire/agent-guide.md for this repository's workflow, source inspection, and verification guidance.\n@.entire/agent-guide.md\n<!-- entire-agent:end -->\n","numLines":73,"startLine":1,"totalLines":73}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"503b084b-049e-46aa-9bc5-6f0abb1c8995","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/changeset-set","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"docs/changeset-set","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
