{"parentUuid":"568eef67-4a8f-41a8-b8fb-0dae629f8af4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CguWFKejHeFUZYzznS7gt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You're right about what private means. `private: true` in npm and `publish = false` in Cargo mean the package is never published to any registry, not that it goes to a private one. So a private package is never a release target.\n\nThat makes the root rule easier to justify, and it keeps the work small:\n- **Private workspace root:** don't discover it, whether or not it has a version. Nothing depends on a root by version, so nothing breaks. This is the one-line extension of the rule that already exists (WS-DISC-08), plus the one-line \"ignored\" note in `status`. No new config.\n- **Other private packages:** they aren't published, and the release PR's \"Merging will publish\" table already leaves them out. I wouldn't remove them from discovery, because a package that others depend on by version, such as a Cargo path dependency with a version or an npm workspace range, still has to move in step with its dependents. Deleting them from the graph would break that.\n\nSo the only \"hiding\" that is safe for non-root private packages is how they're shown. Today `callisto-fixtures` appears in the release summary table and under \"Also bumped\". Marking it private there, or dropping it from the reader-facing lines, is a presentation tweak. I'd skip that unless you find it noisy.\n\nMy recommendation stands: ship the root rule only, as one small PR after did-you-mean.\n\nneeds input: a go on the root rule, and a go on closing the stale trails."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":68,"cache_read_input_tokens":400182,"output_tokens":1783,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1337},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service