17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/changeset-process","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"docs/changeset-process","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"3554e4f2-08ca-49ac-8af8-a25035f9da77","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PostToolUse:Write","toolUseID":"toolu_011fqhqtJ5hsLmtHp5Xihjp7","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"terminalSequence\":\"\\u001b]777;notify;warp://cli-agent;{\\\"v\\\":1,\\\"agent\\\":\\\"claude\\\",\\\"event\\\":\\\"tool_complete\\\",\\\"session_id\\\":\\\"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8\\\",\\\"cwd\\\":\\\"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/changeset-process\\\",\\\"project\\\":\\\"changeset-process\\\",\\\"tool_name\\\":\\\"Write\\\"}\\u0007\"}\n","stderr":"/Users/gabe/.claude/plugins/cache/claude-code-warp/warp/2.1.0/scripts/emit-terminal-sequence.sh: line 82: /dev/tty: Device not configured\n","exitCode":0,"command":"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/on-post-tool-use.sh","durationMs":40},"type":"attachment","uuid":"bdc6d9cc-883a-4729-9753-9cc41b55e4e2","timestamp":"2026-11-10T18:25:53.560Z","session_id":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/changeset-process","sessionId":"6cc3e47b-8406-4c17-94fb-22313682fad8","version":"2.1.311","gitBranch":"docs/changeset-process","slug":"mossy-snacking-stream"}
{"parentUuid":"9755548f-4f1d-49fd-9a93-fac2ec80d04d","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"742a1b36-e83f-484b-a2b0-adb3fe2a079e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01J7KfCP842siXdu2h3jDLDz","type":"tool_result","content":"=========== trail 173\nTrail #173: Specs match the code again after the 2026-11 re-check\nDescription:\nA re-check of all five specs against the code on 2026-11-02 found 20 of 253 criteria drifted (matrix 0 of 24, cli 3 of 55, workspace 2 of 39, versioning 4 of 62, release 11 of 73), down from about 14% in the 2026-10-20 review. Each report was produced by a read-only agent that reproduced behavior with the built binary where it could.\n\nThis trail covers the fixes in the linked branch. Code: completions no longer offer a `help` subcommand (CLI-SURF-06); `init` prints its warnings in text mode (CLI-INIT-14); a private root `package.json` with a byte-order mark is no longer listed as a package (WS-DISC-08); E115 names the real pre-release state file (VER-READ-01); E165 points at the stale-release-PR recovery; in the release-PR script a failed PR read before verification now stops with the `gh` call's own status and a new release PR is created as a draft (REL-PR-08, REL-PR-09, both introduced in #425). Spec corrections where the code is right: WS-LOAD-02, CLI-STATUS-04 and -05, VER-CAS-09, VER-GRP-02, VER-GRP-06, VER-CS-04, RE\n\nNot in this trail, still open as code bugs: `release --dry-run` fails with E170 in a workspace with an artifact and no `origin` (REL-CMD-04, -05); `release plan --from-release-commit` fails with E164 on a shallow clone and with E224 or E164 on a SHA-256 repository before the trust check, not E266 or E265 (REL-AUTH-05); `release plan --artifact-repository` without `--orchestration-revision` is a clap usage error, not E231 or the ignore notice (REL-AUTH-08); the origin tag probe echoes the child process stderr in `release --dry-run` with an unreachable origin (REL-RERUN-08); the JSON error envelope reports `command: release` for `release plan` and omits `dryRun` for release-pr subcommands. Tes\n\nDone when the branch merges with CI green. The open code bugs and test gaps need their own PRs.\n=========== trail 176\nTrail #176: Release previews and preconditions work as specified\nDescription:\nFour bugs found by the 2026-11-02 spec re-check, all in the release command's error and preview paths. Each is a spec criterion the code does not meet.\n\n1. `callisto release --dry-run` fails with E170 (\"requires a GitHub remote\") in a workspace with a `[[release.artifact]]` and no `origin`, because `prepare_remote` demands a remote even for a preview (`derive.rs:317-323`). REL-CMD-04 and REL-CMD-05 say a preview performs no effect, works without `origin`, and prints one note. Fix: in preview, leave upload and forge operations unbound and print the note.\n2. `release plan --from-release-commit` fails with E164 on a shallow clone and with E224 or E164 on a SHA-256 repository, because it parses the commit before the repository trust check. REL-AUTH-05 says every route fails with E266 (shallow) or E265 (not SHA-1). Fix: run the trust check first. No test asserts E265 or E266.\n3. `release plan --package X --artifact-repository o/r` without `--orchestration-revision` is a clap usage error (exit 2, no E-code), because `cli.rs:360` declares `requires`. REL-AUTH-08 says a source without `[release]` ignores both flags and one with it fails with E231. Fix: drop the `requires` so the handler decides.\n4. With an unreachable `origin`, `callisto release --dry-run` prints the version-control tool's fatal message to stderr, because the origin tag probe uses `run_with_timeout`, which echoes the child's stderr (`local.rs:123`). REL-RERUN-08 says nothing is printed. Fix: use the quiet runner and add an end-to-end test for an unreadable remote.\n\nDone when each has a test that fails before the fix and passes after, the specs are unchanged (the code matches them), and the release command's E-codes match `docs/errors.md`.\n=========== trail 177\nTrail #177: Failures report in one consistent shape\nDescription:\nOn failure, `--format json` error output does not follow the envelope that success reports use. `release plan` reports `\"command\": \"release\"`, and the release-pr subcommands report `\"command\": \"release-pr\"` with no `dryRun` (`main.rs:60`, `cli.rs:93`). REL-CMD-12 and REL-PR-07 cover only success reports, so the gap is both in the code and in the specs. The 2026-10-20 review raised the same \"JSON `command` key\" issue for matrix, which is fixed there.\n\nPlan: decide the envelope for errors (the subcommand path as `command`, plus `dryRun`, `schemaVersion`), make every command emit it, extend REL-CMD-12 and REL-PR-07 to say so, and add a test per command family that asserts the error envelope.\n\nDone when a failing `release plan`, `release execute`, `release artifact-manifest`, and each `release-pr` subcommand print the same envelope keys as their success reports, and a test asserts it.\n=========== trail 178\nTrail #178: Every spec criterion has a test that asserts it\nDescription:\nThe 2026-11-02 spec re-check found criteria whose behavior is correct but whose tests are missing or weaker than the claim. Seven to cover, all test-only with no spec or code change:\n\nMX-OUT-02: two cargo artifacts on the same triple sort by `artifactName`; no test. MX-ERR-06: no test for a non-string entry in a target list, and the CLI tests check only non-zero exit, not exit 1 or the path in the message for `engines.node` and `requires-python`. MX-ERR-07: the CLI test checks the package name but not E102. MX-DISC-02: \"neither field means no entry\" is asserted only at the helper level, not end to end. WS-ID-08: E101 (two packages resolving to one tag) has no test. WS-ID-09: E007 has only unit tests, none for the `dynamic = [\"version\"]` case or for failing before `version` and `init` write. VER-CS-03: the byte-order mark and lone carriage return handling has no test.\n\nAlso from the same check, weakly asserted: REL-AUTH-04, REL-AUTH-07, REL-ORD-04, REL-PUB-09, REL-PUB-13, REL-PUB-14, REL-CMD-04, REL-CMD-11, REL-CMD-12, REL-GH-06, REL-GH-04, REL-GH-02, REL-RERUN-03, REL-RERUN-07, REL-RERUN-10, REL-GH-07, REL-PR-02, REL-PR-04, REL-PR-05. Pick these up opportunistically, each only where a real regression could slip through.\n\nDone when the seven named gaps each have a test that fails if the behavior regresses.\n=========== trail 233\nTrail #233: Generated release workflows run in a downstream repo\nDescription:\nThe generated matrix workflow checked out the branch, leaving HEAD attached, so release plan/execute/build-python stopped with E170. Also covers the attestation flags, template pin drift and other generated-output gaps found by the 2026-11 sweeps.\n=========== trail 237\nTrail #237: Cleanup: dead code, stale docs and narration\nDescription:\nRemoves code, error codes and files nothing uses; fixes docs and specs that no longer match the code; replaces history-style comments with present-tense ones; adds a CI guard so they do not return. Findings come from the 2026-11 dead-code and narration sweeps.\n=========== trail 245\nTrail #245: Version writes keep what they should\nDescription:\ncallisto version dropped a root manifest BOM and CRLF, skipped a changelog entry when a heading was merely quoted, left two titles after a rename and rewrote npm override references. Found by the 2026-11 sweeps, each reproduced before the fix.\n=========== trail 246\nTrail #246: A configured registry destination is honored or refused\nDescription:\nA cargo registry url and a yarn npm registry were accepted from callisto.toml and then dropped, so a release could publish to a different registry than configured. Found by the 2026-11 sweeps and reproduced on the real binary.\n=========== trail 198\nTrail #198: Failures are loud and contracts are pinned to real output\nDescription:\nFrom the caliper review patterns applied to callisto on 2026-11-02. Two read-only agents triaged soft failure and one reviewed wire contracts and harness fidelity. Findings are agent-reported and read from the code; only the ones marked checked were confirmed by hand.\nContracts (checked unless noted). (1) create-or-update-release-pr.sh reads jq -r \".action.branch\", and test_release_pr_decide_binary_contract.sh never asserts it, so a rename makes the script try to create or move a branch literally named null. (2) .github/callisto-problem-matcher.json expects error[CODE]: msg then --> file:line:col, but the real binary prints the miette shape (code line, blank, × message, help:), so CI never gets annotations; no test pins it (confirmed by running the binary: agent; matcher read: checked). (3) run-release.sh builds published and publishedPackages with jq over the receipt; a renamed key reports a real publish as not published with exit 0; only a stub receipt \nSoft failure (0 bug, 14 suspect; none reproduced). Both agents say the same thing: an unknown result collapses into a benign one on advisory paths. The unpushed-tag probe returns nothing on any failure and a panic in its thread becomes \"no missing tags\" (release.rs:82,86); the name-taken-by-another-repository check reports clean when the registry or tool failed (capability.rs:537); tag ownership defaults to keep the tag when history cannot be read (tags.rs:283, lib.rs:322); a pipe read error ends capture as if at end of file (runner.rs:423); a stdin write error is dropped (runner.rs:346); rejected tag names are dropped uncounted (vcs/access.rs:540); a workspace: dependency path that does not\nHarness fidelity. The bench (callisto-cli/benches/commands.rs) sets the process-trace variable inside the timed region, uses a real GitHub URL as origin so release --dry-run runs a network lookup, takes the median of three runs with no warm-up and ignores failures in the other two, and never runs release pending, release-pr decide, version --refresh-lockfiles or release plan. The fixture is a single-ecosystem workspace with no [release], no artifacts, no groups and no lockfile.\nPlan: assert .action.branch and fail on null; fix or delete the problem matcher with a test over real stderr; run run-release.sh and move-floating-tag.sh over real binary output; type the pending report; triage each soft-failure site; fix the bench and add the missing commands. Done when each contract a workflow reads has a test over real output and every swallowed error on a release path is either reported or justified in one line.\n=========== trail 151\nTrail #151: CI passes on the new ubuntu-latest image\nDescription:\nProblem: ubuntu-latest moved from 24.04 to 26.04 and every PR now fails the Ubuntu test suite and coverage. The runner test that proves non-Cargo children lose registry tokens builds a program named cargo by symlinking /usr/bin/env, which does not work when env is part of a multi-call coreutils. main itself is green on 24.04, so nothing merged has broken it.\n\nOutcome: the test suite and coverage pass on both the old and the new image without pinning the runner, on main and on every open PR.\n\nDone when: the test no longer depends on how env is installed (the fix PR is merged), and the Ubuntu test suite and coverage jobs pass on the open PRs. Until the fix merges, the same commit is carried on each open PR branch so their CI can run; the duplicate merges cleanly.\n\nWork: #419 (the fix); the same commit is carried on #410 to #418.\n=========== trail 159\nTrail #159: CI and releases run on supported macOS images\nDescription:\nGitHub is retiring the `macos-14` runner image on 2026-11-02, with brownouts in October (date from a search summary of the changelog, not yet confirmed on the changelog page). Jobs pinned to it can queue indefinitely or fail once the image is gone.\n\nCallisto pins `runner: macos-14` in four matrix entries: the `macos-arm64` and `macos-x64` jobs in `.github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml` (artifact preflight) and in `.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml` (release artifact build). The release workflow builds the macOS binaries for the next release, so a missing runner would hang or fail the release that merging #409 starts.\n\nThis trail moves those four entries to `macos-15`, which is arm64 like `macos-14`, so both targets build on the same architecture and `x86_64-apple-darwin` cross-compiles as before. The generated build matrix (`crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs`) already uses `macos-latest` and is unchanged.\n\nDone when CI and the release preflight pass on `macos-15` for both Apple targets and no `macos-14` remains in `.github/`.\n\nNot in scope: re-running the failed Release run on `main` (\"Create or update the Callisto release PR\", a GitHub-side query error that looks transient) and the `macos-latest` test matrix entry.\n\nUnverified: that the Apple jobs pass on `macos-15`; the PR CI is the first evidence.\n=========== trail 160\nTrail #160: CI and releases follow the latest runner images\nDescription:\nCallisto pinned `macos-14` for the macOS artifact jobs, and GitHub retired that image on 2026-11-02. #421 moved the four pins to `macos-15` (trail #159), which would need the same change again when `macos-15` ages out.\n\nThis trail moves the four `runner: macos-15` entries in `.github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml` and `.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml` to `macos-latest`, so every OS label in `.github/` follows the latest image: `ubuntu-latest` was already used everywhere, and the one exception is `ubuntu-24.04-arm`, which has no `-latest` label.\n\nDecision: the owner chose to follow the latest image over pinning a version, accepting that a runner-image change can reach a release build without a PR. The `Release Artifact Preflight` jobs build the same macOS binaries on every PR, so a toolchain or linker break shows up there first. Mac users upgrade quickly and Rust sets its default minimum macOS version independent of the runner, so the minimum-version risk is low; C dependencies built through the `cc` crate are the exception.\n\nDone when both macOS preflight jobs pass on `macos-latest` and no `macos-NN` pin remains in `.github/`. The generated build matrix (`crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs`) already uses `macos-latest` and is unchanged.\n\nNot verified: what `macos-latest` resolves to today; the jobs log the image on each run.\n=========== trail 169\nTrail #169: Entire hooks refresh\nDescription:\nRegenerates the Claude and Codex Entire hook config. Session-start now warns when the entire binary on PATH fails to run, so a broken install no longer silently drops session capture.\n=========== trail 247\nTrail #247: The release pipeline runs verification alongside planning and building\nDescription:\nVerify sat in front of plan and build though only publishing needs it. Splitting out a gate job saves about six minutes of a 32 minute release. Draft until 0.18.0 is released and its timings are read.\n=========== trail 162\nTrail #162: A stale release PR can no longer wedge releases\nDescription:\nEvery push to `main` since 2026-11-02 18:07Z fails the Release workflow at \"Derive and hash intent\" with `E165: no changeset was consumed by this commit`. Nothing was published: no `0.17.0` tag, GitHub release or crates.io version exists, and the latest is `0.16.0`.\n\nWhat happened. Release PR #409 was built from eight generated-name changesets (`gentle-tigers-leap.md` and others). #420 (merged 16:49Z) deleted those eight and added topic-named ones. The release workflow should have refreshed #409 on that push, but its \"Create or update the Callisto release PR\" step failed at 16:57:39Z with `gh: Something went wrong while executing your query` (GitHub request ID 2445:25D0C1:8E695E:1D11C81:6AE8C100), right after printing the 0.17.0 version plan. #409 stayed stale and was merged at 18:07Z. Its eight deletions were already on `main`, so the merge deleted nothing, left every topic changeset pending, and still applied the `0.17.0` bumps, changelogs and `release\n\nCause, in layers. The trigger was a transient GitHub GraphQL error. `create-or-update-release-pr.sh` has no retry on its forge calls (`gh pr list`, the `createCommitOnBranch` mutation, the ref moves, `gh pr edit`), only on the final verify loop, and the workflow runs only on pushes to `main`, so one failed refresh leaves the release PR stale until the next push. Nothing stops a stale release PR from merging: E165 is only raised after the merge, and recovery is a manual revert.\n\nWhich `gh` call failed is not known: the script suppresses stderr on some calls, and the log shows only the final message.\n\nPlan.\n1. Revert the release payload of the #409 merge (PR #423) so the next push regenerates the release PR. Verify the Release workflow turns green and the new release PR consumes every pending changeset.\n2. Retry the forge calls in `create-or-update-release-pr.sh` with bounded backoff, and stop swallowing stderr, so one 5xx does not strand the release PR.\n3. Add a pre-merge guard: a check on the release PR that fails when the merge result still has pending changesets, or when the PR was built from a changeset set that differs from `main`. Specify first, then test, in the release-PR specs.\n4. Write down the E165 recovery (revert the release payload) in the release docs, and decide whether `callisto release pending` should name it.\n5. Separate: `callisto status` warns `tag candidate callisto-graph@0 matched glob but placeholder 0 is not a valid SemVer version`, which suggests stray tags.\n\nDone when the Release workflow is green on `main`, a regenerated release PR consumes the pending changesets, and the retry and guard exist with tests.\n=========== trail 180\nTrail #180: The release PR says why it is held\nDescription:\nThe `Hold release PR` workflow drafts the open release PR on every push to `main`, and the refresh marks it ready only when it was built from the current tip. When the refresh fails, the PR stays a draft with no explanation, so a person sees an unmergeable PR and has to find the failed Release run.\n\nPlan: when the hold workflow drafts a PR, add a short comment that names the push that held it and says the Release workflow will mark it ready after a refresh. When the refresh succeeds, mark it ready and edit that comment. When the refresh fails, edit it to link the failed run. One comment, edited in place, never repeated.\n\nAlso confirm in a real release the first time it runs: the draft-and-restore path, `gh pr ready` and `--undo`, which are unverified against GitHub.\n\nDone when a held release PR carries a comment that states why, a failed refresh links its run, and the first real release shows the full draft-then-ready cycle.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"8d5717b6-2245-4580-9e6b-ab4f2effb94f","timestamp":"2026-11-10T18:26:05.242Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=========== trail 173\nTrail #173: Specs match the code again after the 2026-11 re-check\nDescription:\nA re-check of all five specs against the code on 2026-11-02 found 20 of 253 criteria drifted (matrix 0 of 24, cli 3 of 55, workspace 2 of 39, versioning 4 of 62, release 11 of 73), down from about 14% in the 2026-10-20 review. Each report was produced by a read-only agent that reproduced behavior with the built binary where it could.\n\nThis trail covers the fixes in the linked branch. Code: completions no longer offer a `help` subcommand (CLI-SURF-06); `init` prints its warnings in text mode (CLI-INIT-14); a private root `package.json` with a byte-order mark is no longer listed as a package (WS-DISC-08); E115 names the real pre-release state file (VER-READ-01); E165 points at the stale-release-PR recovery; in the release-PR script a failed PR read before verification now stops with the `gh` call's own status and a new release PR is created as a draft (REL-PR-08, REL-PR-09, both introduced in #425). Spec corrections where the code is right: WS-LOAD-02, CLI-STATUS-04 and -05, VER-CAS-09, VER-GRP-02, VER-GRP-06, VER-CS-04, RE\n\nNot in this trail, still open as code bugs: `release --dry-run` fails with E170 in a workspace with an artifact and no `origin` (REL-CMD-04, -05); `release plan --from-release-commit` fails with E164 on a shallow clone and with E224 or E164 on a SHA-256 repository before the trust check, not E266 or E265 (REL-AUTH-05); `release plan --artifact-repository` without `--orchestration-revision` is a clap usage error, not E231 or the ignore notice (REL-AUTH-08); the origin tag probe echoes the child process stderr in `release --dry-run` with an unreachable origin (REL-RERUN-08); the JSON error envelope reports `command: release` for `release plan` and omits `dryRun` for release-pr subcommands. Tes\n\nDone when the branch merges with CI green. The open code bugs and test gaps need their own PRs.\n=========== trail 176\nTrail #176: Release previews and preconditions work as specified\nDescription:\nFour bugs found by the 2026-11-02 spec re-check, all in the release command's error and preview paths. Each is a spec criterion the code does not meet.\n\n1. `callisto release --dry-run` fails with E170 (\"requires a GitHub remote\") in a workspace with a `[[release.artifact]]` and no `origin`, because `prepare_remote` demands a remote even for a preview (`derive.rs:317-323`). REL-CMD-04 and REL-CMD-05 say a preview performs no effect, works without `origin`, and prints one note. Fix: in preview, leave upload and forge operations unbound and print the note.\n2. `release plan --from-release-commit` fails with E164 on a shallow clone and with E224 or E164 on a SHA-256 repository, because it parses the commit before the repository trust check. REL-AUTH-05 says every route fails with E266 (shallow) or E265 (not SHA-1). Fix: run the trust check first. No test asserts E265 or E266.\n3. `release plan --package X --artifact-repository o/r` without `--orchestration-revision` is a clap usage error (exit 2, no E-code), because `cli.rs:360` declares `requires`. REL-AUTH-08 says a source without `[release]` ignores both flags and one with it fails with E231. Fix: drop the `requires` so the handler decides.\n4. With an unreachable `origin`, `callisto release --dry-run` prints the version-control tool's fatal message to stderr, because the origin tag probe uses `run_with_timeout`, which echoes the child's stderr (`local.rs:123`). REL-RERUN-08 says nothing is printed. Fix: use the quiet runner and add an end-to-end test for an unreadable remote.\n\nDone when each has a test that fails before the fix and passes after, the specs are unchanged (the code matches them), and the release command's E-codes match `docs/errors.md`.\n=========== trail 177\nTrail #177: Failures report in one consistent shape\nDescription:\nOn failure, `--format json` error output does not follow the envelope that success reports use. `release plan` reports `\"command\": \"release\"`, and the release-pr subcommands report `\"command\": \"release-pr\"` with no `dryRun` (`main.rs:60`, `cli.rs:93`). REL-CMD-12 and REL-PR-07 cover only success reports, so the gap is both in the code and in the specs. The 2026-10-20 review raised the same \"JSON `command` key\" issue for matrix, which is fixed there.\n\nPlan: decide the envelope for errors (the subcommand path as `command`, plus `dryRun`, `schemaVersion`), make every command emit it, extend REL-CMD-12 and REL-PR-07 to say so, and add a test per command family that asserts the error envelope.\n\nDone when a failing `release plan`, `release execute`, `release artifact-manifest`, and each `release-pr` subcommand print the same envelope keys as their success reports, and a test asserts it.\n=========== trail 178\nTrail #178: Every spec criterion has a test that asserts it\nDescription:\nThe 2026-11-02 spec re-check found criteria whose behavior is correct but whose tests are missing or weaker than the claim. Seven to cover, all test-only with no spec or code change:\n\nMX-OUT-02: two cargo artifacts on the same triple sort by `artifactName`; no test. MX-ERR-06: no test for a non-string entry in a target list, and the CLI tests check only non-zero exit, not exit 1 or the path in the message for `engines.node` and `requires-python`. MX-ERR-07: the CLI test checks the package name but not E102. MX-DISC-02: \"neither field means no entry\" is asserted only at the helper level, not end to end. WS-ID-08: E101 (two packages resolving to one tag) has no test. WS-ID-09: E007 has only unit tests, none for the `dynamic = [\"version\"]` case or for failing before `version` and `init` write. VER-CS-03: the byte-order mark and lone carriage return handling has no test.\n\nAlso from the same check, weakly asserted: REL-AUTH-04, REL-AUTH-07, REL-ORD-04, REL-PUB-09, REL-PUB-13, REL-PUB-14, REL-CMD-04, REL-CMD-11, REL-CMD-12, REL-GH-06, REL-GH-04, REL-GH-02, REL-RERUN-03, REL-RERUN-07, REL-RERUN-10, REL-GH-07, REL-PR-02, REL-PR-04, REL-PR-05. Pick these up opportunistically, each only where a real regression could slip through.\n\nDone when the seven named gaps each have a test that fails if the behavior regresses.\n=========== trail 233\nTrail #233: Generated release workflows run in a downstream repo\nDescription:\nThe generated matrix workflow checked out the branch, leaving HEAD attached, so release plan/execute/build-python stopped with E170. Also covers the attestation flags, template pin drift and other generated-output gaps found by the 2026-11 sweeps.\n=========== trail 237\nTrail #237: Cleanup: dead code, stale docs and narration\nDescription:\nRemoves code, error codes and files nothing uses; fixes docs and specs that no longer match the code; replaces history-style comments with present-tense ones; adds a CI guard so they do not return. Findings come from the 2026-11 dead-code and narration sweeps.\n=========== trail 245\nTrail #245: Version writes keep what they should\nDescription:\ncallisto version dropped a root manifest BOM and CRLF, skipped a changelog entry when a heading was merely quoted, left two titles after a rename and rewrote npm override references. Found by the 2026-11 sweeps, each reproduced before the fix.\n=========== trail 246\nTrail #246: A configured registry destination is honored or refused\nDescription:\nA cargo registry url and a yarn npm registry were accepted from callisto.toml and then dropped, so a release could publish to a different registry than configured. Found by the 2026-11 sweeps and reproduced on the real binary.\n=========== trail 198\nTrail #198: Failures are loud and contracts are pinned to real output\nDescription:\nFrom the caliper review patterns applied to callisto on 2026-11-02. Two read-only agents triaged soft failure and one reviewed wire contracts and harness fidelity. Findings are agent-reported and read from the code; only the ones marked checked were confirmed by hand.\nContracts (checked unless noted). (1) create-or-update-release-pr.sh reads jq -r \".action.branch\", and test_release_pr_decide_binary_contract.sh never asserts it, so a rename makes the script try to create or move a branch literally named null. (2) .github/callisto-problem-matcher.json expects error[CODE]: msg then --> file:line:col, but the real binary prints the miette shape (code line, blank, × message, help:), so CI never gets annotations; no test pins it (confirmed by running the binary: agent; matcher read: checked). (3) run-release.sh builds published and publishedPackages with jq over the receipt; a renamed key reports a real publish as not published with exit 0; only a stub receipt \nSoft failure (0 bug, 14 suspect; none reproduced). Both agents say the same thing: an unknown result collapses into a benign one on advisory paths. The unpushed-tag probe returns nothing on any failure and a panic in its thread becomes \"no missing tags\" (release.rs:82,86); the name-taken-by-another-repository check reports clean when the registry or tool failed (capability.rs:537); tag ownership defaults to keep the tag when history cannot be read (tags.rs:283, lib.rs:322); a pipe read error ends capture as if at end of file (runner.rs:423); a stdin write error is dropped (runner.rs:346); rejected tag names are dropped uncounted (vcs/access.rs:540); a workspace: dependency path that does not\nHarness fidelity. The bench (callisto-cli/benches/commands.rs) sets the process-trace variable inside the timed region, uses a real GitHub URL as origin so release --dry-run runs a network lookup, takes the median of three runs with no warm-up and ignores failures in the other two, and never runs release pending, release-pr decide, version --refresh-lockfiles or release plan. The fixture is a single-ecosystem workspace with no [release], no artifacts, no groups and no lockfile.\nPlan: assert .action.branch and fail on null; fix or delete the problem matcher with a test over real stderr; run run-release.sh and move-floating-tag.sh over real binary output; type the pending report; triage each soft-failure site; fix the bench and add the missing commands. Done when each contract a workflow reads has a test over real output and every swallowed error on a release path is either reported or justified in one line.\n=========== trail 151\nTrail #151: CI passes on the new ubuntu-latest image\nDescription:\nProblem: ubuntu-latest moved from 24.04 to 26.04 and every PR now fails the Ubuntu test suite and coverage. The runner test that proves non-Cargo children lose registry tokens builds a program named cargo by symlinking /usr/bin/env, which does not work when env is part of a multi-call coreutils. main itself is green on 24.04, so nothing merged has broken it.\n\nOutcome: the test suite and coverage pass on both the old and the new image without pinning the runner, on main and on every open PR.\n\nDone when: the test no longer depends on how env is installed (the fix PR is merged), and the Ubuntu test suite and coverage jobs pass on the open PRs. Until the fix merges, the same commit is carried on each open PR branch so their CI can run; the duplicate merges cleanly.\n\nWork: #419 (the fix); the same commit is carried on #410 to #418.\n=========== trail 159\nTrail #159: CI and releases run on supported macOS images\nDescription:\nGitHub is retiring the `macos-14` runner image on 2026-11-02, with brownouts in October (date from a search summary of the changelog, not yet confirmed on the changelog page). Jobs pinned to it can queue indefinitely or fail once the image is gone.\n\nCallisto pins `runner: macos-14` in four matrix entries: the `macos-arm64` and `macos-x64` jobs in `.github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml` (artifact preflight) and in `.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml` (release artifact build). The release workflow builds the macOS binaries for the next release, so a missing runner would hang or fail the release that merging #409 starts.\n\nThis trail moves those four entries to `macos-15`, which is arm64 like `macos-14`, so both targets build on the same architecture and `x86_64-apple-darwin` cross-compiles as before. The generated build matrix (`crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs`) already uses `macos-latest` and is unchanged.\n\nDone when CI and the release preflight pass on `macos-15` for both Apple targets and no `macos-14` remains in `.github/`.\n\nNot in scope: re-running the failed Release run on `main` (\"Create or update the Callisto release PR\", a GitHub-side query error that looks transient) and the `macos-latest` test matrix entry.\n\nUnverified: that the Apple jobs pass on `macos-15`; the PR CI is the first evidence.\n=========== trail 160\nTrail #160: CI and releases follow the latest runner images\nDescription:\nCallisto pinned `macos-14` for the macOS artifact jobs, and GitHub retired that image on 2026-11-02. #421 moved the four pins to `macos-15` (trail #159), which would need the same change again when `macos-15` ages out.\n\nThis trail moves the four `runner: macos-15` entries in `.github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml` and `.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml` to `macos-latest`, so every OS label in `.github/` follows the latest image: `ubuntu-latest` was already used everywhere, and the one exception is `ubuntu-24.04-arm`, which has no `-latest` label.\n\nDecision: the owner chose to follow the latest image over pinning a version, accepting that a runner-image change can reach a release build without a PR. The `Release Artifact Preflight` jobs build the same macOS binaries on every PR, so a toolchain or linker break shows up there first. Mac users upgrade quickly and Rust sets its default minimum macOS version independent of the runner, so the minimum-version risk is low; C dependencies built through the `cc` crate are the exception.\n\nDone when both macOS preflight jobs pass on `macos-latest` and no `macos-NN` pin remains in `.github/`. The generated build matrix (`crates/callisto-graph/src/matrix.rs`) already uses `macos-latest` and is unchanged.\n\nNot verified: what `macos-latest` resolves to today; the jobs log the image on each run.\n=========== trail 169\nTrail #169: Entire hooks refresh\nDescription:\nRegenerates the Claude and Codex Entire hook config. Session-start now warns when the entire binary on PATH fails to run, so a broken install no longer silently drops session capture.\n=========== trail 247\nTrail #247: The release pipeline runs verification alongside planning and building\nDescription:\nVerify sat in front of plan and build though only publishing needs it. Splitting out a gate job saves about six minutes of a 32 minute release. Draft until 0.18.0 is released and its timings are read.\n=========== trail 162\nTrail #162: A stale release PR can no longer wedge releases\nDescription:\nEvery push to `main` since 2026-11-02 18:07Z fails the Release workflow at \"Derive and hash intent\" with `E165: no changeset was consumed by this commit`. Nothing was published: no `0.17.0` tag, GitHub release or crates.io version exists, and the latest is `0.16.0`.\n\nWhat happened. Release PR #409 was built from eight generated-name changesets (`gentle-tigers-leap.md` and others). #420 (merged 16:49Z) deleted those eight and added topic-named ones. The release workflow should have refreshed #409 on that push, but its \"Create or update the Callisto release PR\" step failed at 16:57:39Z with `gh: Something went wrong while executing your query` (GitHub request ID 2445:25D0C1:8E695E:1D11C81:6AE8C100), right after printing the 0.17.0 version plan. #409 stayed stale and was merged at 18:07Z. Its eight deletions were already on `main`, so the merge deleted nothing, left every topic changeset pending, and still applied the `0.17.0` bumps, changelogs and `release\n\nCause, in layers. The trigger was a transient GitHub GraphQL error. `create-or-update-release-pr.sh` has no retry on its forge calls (`gh pr list`, the `createCommitOnBranch` mutation, the ref moves, `gh pr edit`), only on the final verify loop, and the workflow runs only on pushes to `main`, so one failed refresh leaves the release PR stale until the next push. Nothing stops a stale release PR from merging: E165 is only raised after the merge, and recovery is a manual revert.\n\nWhich `gh` call failed is not known: the script suppresses stderr on some calls, and the log shows only the final message.\n\nPlan.\n1. Revert the release payload of the #409 merge (PR #423) so the next push regenerates the release PR. Verify the Release workflow turns green and the new release PR consumes every pending changeset.\n2. Retry the forge calls in `create-or-update-release-pr.sh` with bounded backoff, and stop swallowing stderr, so one 5xx does not strand the release PR.\n3. Add a pre-merge guard: a check on the release PR that fails when the merge result still has pending changesets, or when the PR was built from a changeset set that differs from `main`. Specify first, then test, in the release-PR specs.\n4. Write down the E165 recovery (revert the release payload) in the release docs, and decide whether `callisto release pending` should name it.\n5. Separate: `callisto status` warns `tag candidate callisto-graph@0 matched glob but placeholder 0 is not a valid SemVer version`, which suggests stray tags.\n\nDone when the Release workflow is green on `main`, a regenerated release PR consumes the pending changesets, and the retry and guard exist with tests.\n=========== trail 180\nTrail #180: The release PR says why it is held\nDescription:\nThe `Hold release PR` workflow drafts the open release PR on every push to `main`, and the refresh marks it ready only when it was built from the current tip. When the refresh fails, the PR stays a draft with no explanation, so a person sees an unmergeable PR and has to find the failed Release run.\n\nPlan: when the hold workflow drafts a PR, add a short comment that names the push that held it and says the Release workflow will mark it ready after a refresh. When the refresh succeeds, mark it ready and edit that comment. When the refresh fails, edit it to link the failed run. One comment, edited in place, never repeated.\n\nAlso confirm in a real release the first time it runs: the draft-and-restore path, `gh pr ready` and `--undo`, which are unverified against GitHub.\n\nDone when a held release PR carries a comment that states why, a failed refresh links its run, and the first real release shows the full draft-then-ready cycle.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"dangerouslyDisableSandbox":true},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ea8321bd-9a42-4acf-b893-698dd6dec09a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/changeset-process","root":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/changeset-process","branch":"docs/changeset-process","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"orin-dx/callisto","visibility":