itions. `resolve.rs` uses it for these cases:\n    - A bad `publish-to` value.\n    - A bad `release-trigger` value.\n    - A cargo registry `url`.\n    - An unparsable package pattern.\n    - Some others at lines 412 to 567.\n    - It also overlaps E111, which now means unknown key. I did not fix it, because splitting it is a larger change.\n  - `ReleaseInvariant` (E171) is used about 45 times, and nearly all look like genuine internal invariants. These look user-reachable through config but are not verified:\n    - `release/binding.rs` ~72 and ~78: an unknown registry, and a `[registries.X]` kind that does not match the publish target's ecosystem.\n    - `release/derive.rs` ~756: \"invalid configured artifact slot\".\n    - Line 72 is probably unreachable. A scratch test for line 78 was blocked by the sandbox. These are the best candidates for a follow-up.\n  - I found no `miette!`, `anyhow!` or `bail!` uses in the crate sources. Variants with no code are covered by the existing architecture test. I did not look further for user-reachable errors with no code.\n  - Other agents may also be allocating codes. I took E325 to E328 as the next free numbers after E324, so check for a collision on merge.\n  \n  **Unsure**\n  - The wrong-type split (E326) goes beyond what you asked for.\n  - I did not run `just ci` or `just pre-push`.\n  \n  **Mishap during the run**\n  - A scratch script of mine ran inside the worktree because `mktemp` failed in the sandbox. It overwrote `callisto.toml`, `Cargo.toml` and `a/`. I restored `callisto.toml` and `Cargo.toml` from HEAD and removed `a/`. A final status check was clean before my edits, and there is no leftover from it.\n</agent-message>","reason":"absorbed_mid_turn","commandUuid":"527e540f-0bfd-4d4f-a794-aaee3f2beb42","deliveryId":"85fa2e3e-2f69-4ac9-8430-059fa50c2758"}
